THE US DOLLAR IS VERY WEAK, 
suffering from "Enron-itis" apparently, as the market become 
aware and/or concerned that the abject evil that had become 
endemic within Enron's senior management may not have 
been confined to Enron, but may have been endemic to 
senior management at other leading US multi-national 
corporations. The revelations that are now coming to the fore 
on a seemingly hourly basis regarding Enron, coupled with 
the revelations last week that the management at Tyco had 
hidden sales of shares through legal but rather untoward and 
highly uncommon  means, and the near utter loss of 
confidence in the accountant's and lawyer's ability to protect 
the public from such corporate evil has send confidence 
plunging... and with it the US dollar. We have always feared 
that the dollar's long bull run would end only when confidence 
ended. We had never feared that the US dollar's ascent 
would end because of a rising imbalance of trade, or 
because of some action taken by the monetary authorities, 
or because of Congressional or Administration actions in the 
political arena. We had never feared these "fundamentals." 
Rather, what we've always feared was some confluence of 
events that would shake confidence, for investors must 
remain confident of where they place their money for 
investment above all else.   Fundamentals follow 
confidence it has always seemed to us, rather than the 
other way around.  Once confidence is lost, like 
virginity, it is very difficult to get it back. The investing 
world is about to lose confidence in the US, and this we 
fear above all else. 
The question is then, ' If global investors lose 
confidence in the US, where then shall they move their 
capital?" Shall Europe enjoy this flight at the margin? 
Shall Japan? Shall China?. Shall it be stocks, or debt 
securities, or gold? We have little confidence in Europe at 
this point, for we still find it hard to believe that Brussels 
shall be a better governing body than shall be Washington. 
We find it immensely difficult to believe that Brussels shall 
suddenly become less bureaucratic and less onerous and 
less intrusive into the lives of its citizens and businesses, 
and for that reason our confidence level in Europe remains 
low. Japan, we fear, is a lost cause due to demographics 
and its government's wholesale inability to deal with reforms 
of even the most modest kind. China and Russia intrigue us, 
but only in relation to the other major countries. 
Debt still seems reasonable in light of deflationary 
tendencies that are still quite clearly extant.  And gold 
intrigues us more and more simply because 
of its historic tendency to rise when 
confidence falls. Perhaps then the easiest 
trade of all is to own debt AND to own gold, 
for certainly the market is telling us loudly in 
the past two weeks or so that that is the 
proper course of action. As we noted here 
last week, we like to watch for anomalies 
that arise, and it is "anomalous" that gold 
and debt are now rising in tandem. 
The revelations of Enron/Tyco/PNC et al will 
prove to be a turning point in the capital 
markets, and not a bullish turn. The public's anger is rising; 
