The US will report factory orders for December later today, 
and we look for orders to have r isen at least 1.0%, or 
perhaps even a bit more than that. This follows November's 
3.3% decline in November, which followed October's rather 
unusually large 7.1% increase.  Prior to October's rise 
(which was of course a technical rebound from the 
precipitous decline in September), industrial production fell in 
September, in August, in July and in June... evidence of the 
onset of recession. 
COMMODITY PRICES ARE VERY 
SLIGHTLY WEAKER, but the precious metals 
(and especially gold) are firmer, as capital is fleeing to gold 
as it flees other areas of investment. Although the advance 
in gold (and gold shares) has become almost parabolic and 
therefore a bit worrisome, weakness is to be awaited and 
bought rather than strength sold into. This is a sea-change 
in our outlook, and it may colour the manner in which we 
trade for a very, very long while into the future. 
Turning our attention elsewhere for a moment, we apparently 
angered a large number of our agricultural clients last week 
and the week before when we suggested that wheat prices 
had risen a bit too swiftly and would likely weaken following 
some increased moisture and snow coverage in parts of the 
winter wheat growing areas of the central US. Further, we 
feared that the then strong US dollar, as well as the 
collapsing Argentine peso, might well draw export demand 
