Russian oil production is now the focus : How much are the 
Russian oil companies producing; are they really abiding 
OPEC's quotas; and how much will they be producing in the 
future? The answers to these questions are simple: For the 
first: "More than is thought, by a large margin." For the 
second: emphatically "NO!" And for the third: "A very large 
amount indeed." According to Petroleumworld 
despite a pledge to cut output by 150,000 bpd for 
the first three months of the year, Russia is 
expected to raise crude production in 2002 
by 440,000 bpd to 7.46 million bpd, the International 
Energy Agency says. 
Russia's oil production cut was specious at best, for 
Russia's production and exports always fall during the winter 
months as her exporting ports are all but closed due to 
freezing weather and as their producing regions close for 
even colder temperatures. They increase again in the spring 
when these ports re-open and when their production facilities 
do the same. By April or May, production will be right back 
to where it was at the end of last year, and by June almost 
certainly it will be higher. By the end of the year, production 
will be 440,000 barrels more, with the very vast majority of 
this being exported. This is hardly a bullish case for crude 
oil, unless demand globally ratchets up far more swiftly than 
even the most optimistic analyst proposes. 
Itar-Tass, the Russian news agency, is reporting that 
Russian Prime Minister Kasyanov has told President Bush 
that his country may soon consider rescinding its crude oil 
export limits... perhaps as soon as the 2nd quarter. This 
shall be enormously bearish news for crude oil and it is 
indeed a clear warning to OPEC that Russia shall watch out 
for its own best economic and political interests. We found 
it almost comical that the oil trading world considered 
Russia's "commitment" to OPEC to cut production by 
150,000 bpd to be anything other than a calendrical (and very 
temporary) circumstance: .         
