ON THE POLITICAL FRONT the first polls 
after President Bush's State of the Union Address are in and 
the Democrats must be in a very real state of denial, for his 
ratings have increased in all areas. We suspect that many 
on the centre-left had hoped that President Bush finally 
would suffer some material rhetorical break-down during his 
speech... that he would make some very public rhetorical 
gaff that they could exploit; or that he would make some 
policy statement that they could find fault with. He did not, 
and they have not, and this must be enormously frustrating 
to them.Worse yet for the centre-left, according to an 
ABCNews/Washington Post poll, in light of the President's 
high ratings, 63% of those polled have a favourable opinion of 
the Republican Party while 30% have an unfavourable opinion 
of it. At the same time, while 56% have a favourable opinion 
of the Democrats, 36% have an unfavourable view. This is a 
material   shift   from   a   year   ago   when   the 
favourable/unfavourable ratings were always and everywhere 
in the Democrat Party's favour.  Worse yet, when polled, 
37% have a favourable opinion of Sen. Majority leader 
Daschle; 31% view him unfavourably. Mrs. Bush remains 
enormously popular with favourable ratings of 81% vs. 
unfavourable ratings of only 8%. 
However, we do believe that the President has erred badly in 
his "Evil Axis" commentary of last week, especially in his  
comments regarding Iran. The President has tied Iran and 
Iraq together in the mind of the American people, and that is 
decidedly wrong. He has sent a message of non-hope to 
those reformist elements (including President Khatami) 
within the Iranian government and populace and that the US 
views their efforts toward reform as meaningless. Iran and 
Iraq are enemies, not "allies," and it is in the US and the 
West's interests to invite and encourage that enmity. 
Instead, for wont of a rhetorical flourish, the President has 
made an error that will not be readily salved. 
In Nigeria, tribal warfare is raging... and it is doing 
so in the worst of all possible places: in Lagos, the nation's 
largest and most important city. The Muslim Hausa are 
battling the nominally Christian/Animist Yoruba, with the 
fighting having resulted in the deaths of at least 55 people 
since Saturday and with injuries now in the many hundreds. 
There are horrifying reports of Hausa attacking Yoruba with 
machetes and hatchets, just as there are reports of the 
Yoruba doing the same to the Hausa. Each tribe blames the 
other for the violence, with the police powerless to stop the 
tribal warfare. We've seen this before in Africa. Witness the 
horrifying violence in Rwanda and Burundi over several years 
ago, resulting in the deaths of hundreds of thousands as 
tribe waged war upon tribe. 
Worse still in Nigeria, the Army is standing by watching, as 
there are reports of a potential military coup against 
President Obasanjo, with the latter unwilling to call the army 
out, fearing just such a coup. Nigeria has always been a 
nation of tribal antagonism, but normally it has been confined 
to the smaller tribes in the states far from Lagos and even 
from Abuja. When the Hausa-Folani and the Yoruba, the two 
largest tribes by far,  do battle with one another in the 
nation's largest city, this is tribal warfare at a new and very 
uncomfortable level. 
COMMENTS ON 
THE CAPITAL MARKETS 
MORE ON THE IDIOCY THAT IS 
ARGENTINE ECONOMICS: We take the IMF 
to task rather often for the economic idiocy that passed for 
economic wisdom residing there. As we have said, the IMF' 
policies of devaluation and increased taxes have led to vast 
international economic calamities.  Of the IMF's client 
states, one is very hard put to come up with one nation that 
is better off for having had the IMF apply its economic magic; 
one is rather easily given to finding those far the worse for 
the IMF's wear. 
Ah, but the IMF has no lock on economic idiocy. Argentina 
seems now to be virtual font of such nonsense. Let us note, 
for example, that the newly appointed Governor of the Central 
Bank of Argentina, Mr. Mario Blejer, who is either incredibly 
naive or is a liar. He said recently that it is obvious that 
central bankers can print money as fast as they wish but 
that he will not do that. Rather, Blejer has said that he 
intends to run an "independent" but  "very responsible" 
central bank. Mr. Blejer, we supposed, truly believes that he 
has been given the assurance by his president, Mr. Duhalde, 
that his central bank independence will be maintained. 
However, last week, Mr. Duhalde's appointed Vice Minister 
of Finance, Mr. Jorge Todesca, said that the central bank 
has every right to print more Argentine peso because the 
value of the Central Bank's reserves have gone up by 40% 
following the peso's devaluation. This is utter nonsense of 
course, but Todesca believes it. Further, Mr. Todesca said 
that the policy on printing peso's "will be defined by the 
Economy Ministry....." Blejer's assurances to the contrary. 
THE CRIMINALITY WAS ENDEMIC, 
WIDE-SPREAD AND ENORMOUS: The 
"hits" just keep on coming regarding Enron, the latest of 
which is the report by a special committee appointed by 
Enron's board of directors that shows just how egregious 
was the rapacity and hubris of Mr. Fastow (and others) within 
the company's management in setting up self-dealing third 
party corporations and partnerships that dealt directly with 
the parent company. According to a report in The New 
York Times 
one transaction was cited as being of particular 
concern. According to the report, Fastow appears 
to have offered a group of Enron employees the 
opportunity to invest in a partnership called 
Southampton Place, which provided spectacular 
returns. In about two months, a $25,000 investment 
in Southampton netted Fastow $4.5 million. Two 
other employees, who invested just $5,800, 
received $1.0 million in the same period. 
This is better even that Ms. Clinton's cattle trading 
operations of a decade ago. Sadly, given that there were 
hundreds of such off-balance sheet, special purpose 
vehicles, all of the facts surrounding this situation have not 
yet come to the light of day. Southampton may be the most 
egregious of them, but rest assured there will be dozens 
upon dozens of others that benefited Fastow & Company, to 
the detriment of the company's employees and shareholders. 
If this is not criminal, we don't know what is. If an 18 year 
old black youth can get 10 years for a $100 robbery, what 
then is the fitting punishment for greed on this order? 
Very late yesterday afternoon, the newly appointed member 
of Enron's Board, Prof. Powers of the University of Texas, the 
author of this report, appeared before Congress to tell those 
assembled of what he had found out. He minced non words, 
and he has done the country much good in point out just 
how serious and how far-reaching and how endemic and how 
severe  was  the  corruption  within  Enron's  senior  
management. Powers left nothing to the imagination, making 
it quite clear that Fastow was the centre of the corruption; 
that Skilling knew about the corruption and that Lay too 
understood what was taking place and allowed it to continue. 
We too shall mince no words; these gentleman were 
criminal; they have done immense and perhaps irreparable 
damage to the capital markets; they've stolen money from 
the company and from its shareholders and from its current 
and future retirees. If what Powers said is true, these men 
(and apparently one or two women at the company) must go 
to jail...and for a very long while [Ed. Note: We understand 
from several news reports late yesterday that Mr. Fastow's 
lawyers are already seeking a plea bargain, trying to keep 
his potential jail time to a minimum, exchange for his 
testimony that would obviously implicate others within the 
company. We shall hope that the federal prosecutors will 
not accept that plea, for what is worse than having he at the 
very centre of the corruption "rat out" his associates. Even 
the Mafia won't do that; even the Yakuza won't do that. 
Fastow apparently will.]. 
